# Loadly Project - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is a Vue 3 + TypeScript multi-dashboard application with the following architecture:
- **Frameworks**: Vue 3 (Composition API), TypeScript, Pinia, Vue Router, VeeValidate, Yup
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS v4
- **Build Tool**: Vite
- **Multi-Platform**: admin-dashboard, app-dashboard, company-dashboard

## Project Structure

### Directory Organization
```
src/
├── assets/          # CSS, fonts, images, SVG
│   ├── css/        # Base, components, fonts, theme, utility styles
│   ├── fonts/      # Font files (ar/, en/)
│   ├── images/     # Image assets
│   └── svg/        # SVG assets
├── components/     # Reusable Vue components (auto-imported)
│   ├── base/       # Base form components (BaseInput, BaseSelect, BasePopup, etc.)
│   ├── card/       # Card components (CardPreview, CardPreviewLabel, CardAnalyticsCounter)
│   ├── icon/       # Icon components (IconUser, IconCheck, etc.)
│   ├── navigation/ # Navigation components (NavigationMenu, NavigationTabs, NavigationItem)
│   ├── popup/      # Popup components (PopupError, PopupSuccess, etc.)
│   └── uploader/   # File upload components (Uploader, UploaderItem, etc.)
├── composables/    # Reusable composition functions
│   ├── admin-dashboard/  # Platform-specific composables
│   └── ui/         # UI utilities (useSelectDropdown, etc.)
├── layouts/        # Layout components organized by platform
├── locale/         # i18n translation files (platform-specific: button, content, error, form, sidebar, success, table, title)
├── router/         # Router configuration by platform
│   └── {platform}/guards/  # Navigation and permission guards
├── stores/         # Pinia stores organized by platform
├── types/          # TypeScript type definitions
│   ├── admin-dashboard/
│   │   ├── request/   # Request type definitions
│   │   └── response/  # Response type definitions
│   ├── request/    # Shared request types
│   └── response/   # Shared response types (pagination, lookup, enum)
├── utils/          # Utility functions
│   ├── api/        # API utilities and interceptors (platform-specific)
│   └── validation/ # Validation utilities
└── views/          # Page components organized by platform
    └── errors/     # Shared error pages (NotFound, Unauthorized)
```

## Code Patterns & Conventions

### Vue Components

#### Component Structure
- Always use `<script setup lang="ts">` for Vue components
- Use `defineOptions({ inheritAttrs: false })` for base components that need to control attribute inheritance
- Use TypeScript generics for `defineProps` and `defineEmits`:
  ```typescript
  const props = defineProps<{
    formValues?: Record<string, unknown>
    errors?: Record<string, string[]>
  }>()
  
  const emit = defineEmits<{
    update: [data: Record<string, unknown>]
    next: []
  }>()
  ```
- **CRITICAL - defineEmits Usage:**
  - Only assign `defineEmits` to a variable (e.g., `const emit = defineEmits<...>()`) if you need to call `emit()` in the script section
  - If you're only using `$emit()` in templates (inline emits), use `defineEmits<{...}>()` without assignment
  - Example:
    ```typescript
    // ✅ CORRECT: No assignment when only using $emit() in template
    defineEmits<{
      setPage: [page: number]
      nextPage: []
    }>()
    
    // ✅ CORRECT: Assignment when calling emit() in script
    const emit = defineEmits<{
      update: [data: Record<string, unknown>]
    }>()
    
    function handleUpdate() {
      emit('update', data)
    }
    ```

#### Component Naming
- Use PascalCase for component names: `ContractInformation.vue`
- Use kebab-case for component file names matching the component name
- Base components should be prefixed with `Base`: `BaseInput.vue`, `BaseForm.vue`
- Icon components should be prefixed with `Icon`: `IconUser.vue`, `IconCheck.vue`

#### Template Patterns
- Use Tailwind CSS classes for styling
- Prefer composition over configuration when possible
- Use slots for flexible component composition
- Use `v-slot` with named slots for form field customization

### TypeScript Conventions

#### Type Definitions
- Store types in appropriate directories:
  - Request types: `src/types/{platform}/request/`
  - Response types: `src/types/{platform}/response/`
- Use interfaces for object shapes
- Use type aliases for unions and complex types
- Export adapters alongside types in the same file

#### Naming Conventions
- Functions: `camelCase` (enforced by ESLint)
- Components: `PascalCase`
- Constants: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` or `camelCase` depending on context
- Types/Interfaces: `PascalCase`
- Private functions can start with underscore: `_helperFunction`

#### Type Adapters Pattern
- Always create request adapters to transform frontend data to API format
- Always create response adapters to transform API data to frontend format
- Adapters should be exported from the same file as the types
- Example:
  ```typescript
  export function companyCreateRequestAdapter(data: CompanyCreateRequest): FormData {
    const formData = new FormData()
    // transformation logic
    return formData
  }
  
  export function companyAdapter(company: ApiCompany): Company {
    return {
      // transformation logic
    }
  }
  ```

#### Handling Bilingual Content in Response Adapters
- **CRITICAL**: When API responses contain bilingual fields (e.g., `nameEn`/`nameAr`, `descEn`/`descAr`), follow this pattern:
  1. **API Interface**: Keep separate bilingual fields in the API interface (e.g., `ApiContractType`):
     ```typescript
     export interface ApiContractType {
       nameEn: string
       nameAr: string
       descEn: string
       descAr: string
       // ... other fields
     }
     ```
  2. **Frontend Interface**: Use single localized fields in the frontend interface (e.g., `ContractType`):
     ```typescript
     export interface ContractType {
       name: string
       description: string
       // ... other fields (omit unnecessary fields like addedBy, createdAt, updatedAt if not needed)
     }
     ```
  3. **Adapter Function**: Use `getLocalizedValue()` utility to combine bilingual fields:
     ```typescript
     export function contractTypeAdapter(data: ApiContractType): ContractType {
       return {
         id: data.id,
         name: getLocalizedValue(data.nameEn, data.nameAr),
         description: getLocalizedValue(data.descEn, data.descAr),
         bestFor: getLocalizedValue(data.bestForEn, data.bestForAr),
         status: data.status,
         // Omit fields not needed in frontend (addedBy, createdAt, updatedAt, etc.)
       }
     }
     ```
  - **Benefits**:
    - Cleaner frontend interfaces with single fields instead of duplicate bilingual fields
    - Automatic language selection based on user's locale
    - Removes unnecessary fields that aren't used in the UI
    - Consistent pattern across all bilingual content
  - **Response Property Naming**: Use concise property names in response interfaces (e.g., `types` instead of `contractTypes` when the context is clear)

### Composables Pattern

#### Structure
- Composables should be in `src/composables/` or `src/composables/{platform}/`
- Use the `use` prefix: `useCompanyInformation.ts`, `useVeeValidate.ts`
- Export a single function that returns an object with all needed values/functions
- Use `useVeeValidate()` when working with forms
- Example:
  ```typescript
  export function useCompanyInformation() {
    const { t } = useI18n()
    const companyStore = useCompanyStore()
    const { companiesListAsLookup } = storeToRefs(companyStore)
    
    useVeeValidate()
    
    // functions and computed values
    
    return {
      companiesListAsLookup,
      fieldsSchema,
      initialValues,
      validationSchema,
    }
  }
  ```

### Pinia Stores Pattern

#### Store Structure
- Stores should be in `src/stores/{platform}/`
- Use `defineStore` with composition API syntax
- Store reactive state with `ref` or `computed`
- Use `storeToRefs` when destructuring reactive state from stores
- API calls should use the `api` utility from `@/utils/api`
- Always use request/response adapters in store methods
- Example:
  ```typescript
  export const useCompanyStore = defineStore('company', () => {
    const companiesList = ref<CompaniesListResponse | null>(null)
    
    async function getList(data: CompaniesListRequest) {
      return api({
        url: '/companies',
        method: 'GET',
        params: companiesListRequestAdapter(data),
        responseAdapter: companiesListResponseAdapter,
      })
    }
    
    return {
      companiesList,
      getList,
    }
  })
  ```

### Router Patterns

#### Route Organization
- Routes are organized by domain in separate files: `companies.ts`, `users.ts`, `contracts.ts`, etc.
- Each route file exports a `{feature}Routes` array
- Import and combine in `src/router/admin-dashboard/index.ts`

#### Route Configuration
- Use layout wrapper: `component: () => import('@/layouts/defaultLayout.vue')`
- Use lazy loading for views: `component: () => import('@/views/...')`
- Route naming: `{Feature}List`, `Create{Feature}`, `Update{Feature}`
- Route meta must include: `title`, `requiresAuth`, `module` (Permission.*), `permission` (Resource.Action)

#### Route Meta
```typescript
meta: {
  title: 'Companies',           // Used for document.title
  requiresAuth: true,
  module: Permission.Company,   // From @/types/admin-dashboard/response/permission-group
  permission: Company.View,     // Resource-specific permission
  isMfaStep?: boolean,         // For MFA flow routes
}
```

#### Navigation Guards
- Admin dashboard uses `navigationGuard` in `router.beforeEach`
- Use `checkPermissionGuard(requiredPermission, requiredModule)` for permission checks
- Unauthorized users redirect to `/errors/unauthorized`
- Unauthenticated users redirect to `/auth/login`

### Permission Pattern

- Import `Permission` and resource constants from `@/types/admin-dashboard/response/permission-group`
- Use `usePermission(module, permission)` composable for permission checks in components
- Check `hasPermission` and `hasModule` for conditional UI (e.g., hide Create button)
- Example: `const { hasCreatePermission } = usePermission(Permission.Company, Company.Create)`

### Popup Pattern

- Use `defineModel<boolean>('modelValue', { required: true })` for open/close state
- Wrap content with `BasePopup` (or extend PopupError, PopupSuccess, etc.)
- Use `v-model` for two-way binding: `v-model="isCreatePopupOpen"`
- Popups use `Teleport to="body"` (handled by BasePopup)
- For form popups: emit `@refresh` to parent to refetch data after success

### List Page Pattern

- Create `useList` composable in `composables/useList.ts`
- Use `usePagination(paginationRef)` for pagination controls (setPage, nextPage, prevPage, updateItemsPerPage)
- Use `useLoading()` for loading state during fetch
- Transform API pagination to unified format: `{ page, size, pages, items }` (see `Pagination` type)
- Use `watch` on `currentPage` and `itemsPerPage` to trigger `fetch{C_resource}`
- Return: `{ items, fetchItems, isLoading, pagination, setPage, nextPage, prevPage, updateItemsPerPage }`
- List page structure: header with title + action button, table/cards, Create/Edit/Delete popups

### Pagination Pattern

- API returns `ApiPagination`: `{ page, pageSize, totalCount, totalPages }`
- Frontend uses `Pagination`: `{ page, size, pages, items }`
- Use `apiPaginationAdapter()` from `@/types/response/pagination` when needed
- `usePagination()` accepts `Ref<Pagination | null>` or `ComputedRef<Pagination | null>`

### API Patterns

#### API Utility Usage
- Always use the `api` utility from `@/utils/api`
- Include `requestAdapter` for transforming request data
- Include `responseAdapter` for transforming response data
- Handle FormData appropriately (api utility handles it automatically)
- Example:
  ```typescript
  return api({
    url: '/companies',
    method: 'POST',
    data: companyCreateRequestAdapter(data),
    responseAdapter: companyResponseAdapter,
  })
  ```

#### Lookup API Pattern
- **CRITICAL**: When adding new lookup endpoints (e.g., `/lookups/{resource}/default`), follow this pattern:
  1. **Add to Lookup Store** (`src/stores/lookup.ts`):
     - Add a `ref<LookupResponse>` for the lookup data (e.g., `const companies = ref<LookupResponse>({} as LookupResponse)`)
     - Create a `get{Resource}` function that:
       - Uses `API_BASE_URL + '/{resource}/default'`
       - Accepts `LookupRequest` as parameter
       - Uses `lookupRequestAdapter(params)` for request transformation
       - Uses `lookupResponseAdapter` for response transformation (if API follows standard lookup format)
       - Returns the API response
     - Export the ref and function in the store return object
  2. **Add to useLookups Composable** (`src/composables/useLookups.ts`):
     - Add the ref to `storeToRefs` destructuring
     - Create a `get{Resource}` function that calls the store method and updates the ref
     - Export the ref and function in the composable return object
  3. **Use in Components/Composables**:
     - Always use `useLookups()` composable instead of directly accessing the lookup store
     - Destructure the needed lookup data and functions: `const { companies, getCompanies } = useLookups()`
     - Call the getter function when needed: `await getCompanies(page)`
     - Access items via the ref: `companies.value?.items ?? []`
  - **Example Implementation**:
    ```typescript
    // In lookup store
    const companies = ref<LookupResponse>({} as LookupResponse)
    
    function getCompanies(params: LookupRequest) {
      return api({
        url: API_BASE_URL + '/companies/default',
        method: 'GET',
        params: lookupRequestAdapter(params),
        responseAdapter: lookupResponseAdapter,
      })
    }
    
    // In useLookups composable
    const { companies } = storeToRefs(lookupStore)
    
    async function getCompanies(page?: number): Promise<void> {
      const { data } = await lookupStore.getCompanies({ page })
      companies.value = data
    }
    
    // In component/composable
    const { companies, getCompanies } = useLookups()
    await getCompanies(1)
    const options = companies.value?.items ?? []
    ```
  - **Why this pattern?**
    - Provides consistent interface across all lookups
    - Centralizes lookup logic in one composable
    - Makes it easy to add pagination, loading states, and other features
    - Follows DRY principle by reusing `lookupRequestAdapter` and `lookupResponseAdapter`

### Form Patterns

#### Form Schema
- Use the `Schema` and `FieldsGroup` interfaces from `@/types/form` for field definitions
- Schema properties: `as`, `name`, `label`, `labelRequired`, `options`, `containerClass`, `fieldSlot`, `group`, etc.
- Define `fieldsSchema` as a computed property
- Use `BaseForm` component with schema-driven approach
- Include validation schema using Yup
- Example:
  ```typescript
  const validationSchema = object({
    companyId: string().required(),
    startDate: string().required(),
  })
  
  const fieldsSchema = computed<Schema[]>(() => [
    {
      name: 'companyId',
      as: 'BaseSelect',
      options: companiesListAsLookup.value?.items ?? [],
      label: t('form.selectCompany'),
      labelRequired: true,
      containerClass: 'w-full',
    },
  ])
  ```

#### Validation
- Use Yup for validation schemas
- Always call `useVeeValidate()` in composables that handle forms
- Use custom validation utilities from `@/utils/validation/`
- Validation messages should be internationalized via `t('validation.*')`

### Styling Conventions

#### Stylelint
- Project uses Stylelint for CSS/Vue style linting (`stylelint.config.mjs`)
- Supports Tailwind at-rules: `@theme`, `@custom-variant`, `@apply`, etc.
- See `documentation/linters.md` for full configuration

#### Tailwind CSS
- Use Tailwind utility classes exclusively
- Follow the project's Tailwind class ordering (enforced by ESLint)
- Use responsive prefixes: `md:`, `lg:`, etc.
- Use semantic color classes: `text-neutral-10`, `bg-error-600`, etc.
- Common patterns:
  - Input height: `h-11`
  - Input border radius: `rounded-lg`
  - Spacing: Use `gap-*`, `mt-*`, `mb-*`, `p-*` consistently
  - Flexbox: `flex`, `flex-col`, `items-center`, `justify-between`

#### Component Styling
- Use `scoped` styles when needed
- Prefer Tailwind classes over custom CSS
- Use CSS variables for theme values when appropriate

### Internationalization (i18n)

#### Translation Keys
- Use namespaced keys: `form.fieldName`, `validation.ruleName`, `a11y.actionName`, `title.*`, `button.*`, etc.
- Admin-dashboard locale files: `button.en.ts`, `content.en.ts`, `error.en.ts`, `form.en.ts`, `sidebar.en.ts`, `success.en.ts`, `table.en.ts`, `title.en.ts`
- Always use `useI18n()` composable: `const { t } = useI18n()`
- Store translations in `src/locale/{platform}/` directories
- Use `getLocalizedValue()` utility for bilingual content (English/Arabic)
- **CRITICAL**: When adding new translation keys in components, you MUST immediately add the corresponding translations to the appropriate locale files:
  - Content keys → `src/locale/{platform}/content.en.ts`
  - Button keys → `src/locale/{platform}/button.en.ts`
  - Form keys → `src/locale/{platform}/form.en.ts`
  - Error keys → `src/locale/{platform}/error.en.ts`
  - Title keys → `src/locale/{platform}/title.en.ts`
  - Add translations in alphabetical order within the file
  - If Arabic translations exist, add them to corresponding `.ar.ts` files as well
- **CRITICAL - Duplicate Prevention**: Before adding a new translation key, ALWAYS check if the key already exists in the target locale file. Use `grep` or search the file to verify the key doesn't already exist. Never add duplicate translation keys.

### File Organization

#### Views
- Organize views by platform: `src/views/{platform}/`
- Group related components in subdirectories
- Use composables for view logic: `src/views/{platform}/{feature}/composables/`
- Keep components close to views: `src/views/{platform}/{feature}/components/`

#### View Feature Patterns
- **List pages**: `{feature}/list/` with `{Feature}List.vue`, `components/`, `composables/useList.ts`
- **Create pages**: `{feature}/create/` with `Create{Feature}.vue`, `components/`, `composables/`
- **Update pages**: `{feature}/update/` with `Update{Feature}.vue`, `components/`, `composables/`
- **Wizard flows** (multi-step forms): `{feature}/wizard/` with `{Feature}Wizard.vue`, `components/`, `composables/use{Feature}Wizard.ts`
- **Settings CRUD**: `settings/{resource}/` with `{Resource}List.vue`, Create/Edit/Delete popups in `components/`

#### Imports
- Use `@/` alias for `src/` directory
- Group imports: Vue imports, then composables, then stores, then types, then utils
- Use type imports: `import type { ... }`
- **Auto-imported** (do NOT import): `ref`, `computed`, `watch`, `onMounted`, etc. from Vue; `useRouter`, `useRoute` from Vue Router; `storeToRefs` from Pinia; `useI18n` from vue-i18n. Configured via `unplugin-auto-import` in vite.config.ts.
- **CRITICAL - Component Imports:**
  - **DO NOT import** components from `src/components/` directory (including subdirectories like `base/`, `icon/`, `popup/`). These components are auto-imported via `unplugin-vue-components` configured in `vite.config.ts`. Simply use the component name directly in templates without explicit imports.
  - **DO import** local/feature-specific components from `src/views/{platform}/{feature}/components/` directories. These are NOT auto-imported and require explicit imports.
  - Example:
    ```typescript
    // ✅ CORRECT: Import local component
    import VehicleTypeCard from './VehicleTypeCard.vue'
    
    // ❌ WRONG: Don't import global components
    // import BaseRadio from '@/components/base/BaseRadio.vue'
    // import IconPlus from '@/components/icon/IconPlus.vue'
    ```

### Code Quality Rules

#### ESLint Rules (Enforced)
- No empty blocks (except catch with `allowEmptyCatch: true`)
- No debugger statements
- Console allowed only for `info`, `warn`, `error`
- Blank line before return statements
- Vue script blocks must use `lang="ts"`
- Functions must use camelCase
- Tailwind class ordering and consistency rules

#### TypeScript
- Use strict TypeScript configuration
- Always type function parameters and return types
- Use `unknown` over `any` when type is truly unknown
- Prefer interfaces for object shapes

#### Best Practices
- Always handle async operations with proper error handling
- Use `ref` for reactive primitives, `reactive` for objects (prefer `ref`)
- Use `computed` for derived state
- Use `watch` and `watchEffect` appropriately
- Clean up side effects in `onUnmounted` when needed
- Use `onMounted` for initialization logic

### Common Commands & Workflows

#### Development
- `npm run dev` - Run all dashboards in parallel
- `npm run dev:admin` - Run admin dashboard only
- `npm run dev:app` - Run app dashboard only
- `npm run dev:company` - Run company dashboard only
- `npm run lint` - Lint and fix code (ESLint)
- `npm run type-check` - Type check TypeScript

#### Build
- `npm run build` - Runs lint, then builds all three platforms
- Build output directories: `dist/admin-dashboard`, `dist/app-dashboard`, `dist/company-dashboard`

#### Git Hooks (Husky)
- `commit-msg`: Commitlint validates conventional commit format
- Run `npm run prepare` to install Husky hooks

#### Creating New Features

1. **New Form/Page:**
   - Create view component in appropriate platform directory
   - Create composable for form logic (validation, initial values, fields schema)
   - Create/use store for API calls
   - Define request/response types with adapters
   - Use `BaseForm` with schema-driven approach

2. **New Store:**
   - Create store in `src/stores/{platform}/`
   - Define request/response types with adapters
   - Use `api` utility for all API calls
   - Export reactive state and methods

3. **New Component:**
   - Create in appropriate directory (`components/base/`, `components/icon/`, or view-specific)
   - Use `<script setup lang="ts">`
   - Type all props and emits
   - Use Tailwind for styling

4. **New Composable:**
   - Create in `src/composables/` or `src/composables/{platform}/`
   - Use `use` prefix
   - Return object with all needed values/functions
   - Call `useVeeValidate()` if working with forms

### Commit Conventions

- Use **Conventional Commits** (enforced by Commitlint + Husky)
- Format: `<type>: <subject>`
- Allowed types: `build`, `chore`, `ci`, `docs`, `feat`, `fix`, `perf`, `refactor`, `revert`, `style`, `test`
- Examples: `feat: add company creation form`, `fix: resolve login redirect issue`

### Key Utilities Reference

- `getLocalizedValue(enValue, arValue)` - From `@/utils/i18n.ts`, returns value based on current locale (ar/en)
- `Platform` enum and `isPlatform()` - From `@/types/platform` for platform detection
- `Pagination` / `ApiPagination` / `apiPaginationAdapter` - From `@/types/response/pagination`

### Platform-Specific Considerations

#### Multi-Dashboard Architecture
- Code should be organized by platform when platform-specific
- Use `import.meta.env.MODE` to determine current platform
- Platform enum: `Platform.ADMIN_DASHBOARD`, `Platform.APP_DASHBOARD`, `Platform.COMPANY_DASHBOARD`
- Routes are loaded dynamically via `routesResolver.ts` based on MODE (only one platform's routes per build)
- API interceptors are platform-specific in `src/utils/api/interceptors/`
- Environment: `VITE_API_BASE_URL` for API base URL

### Error Handling

#### API Errors
- API utility returns `{ success, message, data, errors }`
- Always check `success` before using `data`
- Handle `errors` object for field-level validation errors
- Use error popups for user-facing errors

#### Form Validation
- Use Yup schemas for client-side validation
- Display server-side errors via `errors` prop on `BaseForm`
- Use error classes: `text-error-600` for error text

### Testing Considerations

- Write composables to be easily testable
- Keep business logic in composables, not components
- Use stores for API interactions (easier to mock)

### Breadcrumb Usage

- Use `BaseBreadcrumb` component with `breadcrumbItems` array for page navigation context
- Typically placed at top of list/detail pages
- Define breadcrumb items in composable or component

### API Request/Response Adapters

- `requestAdapter` signature: `(data, headers: AxiosHeaders) => data`
- `responseAdapter` signature: `(data) => transformedData`
- API returns `{ success, message, data, errors }` - always check `success` before using `data`

## Code Review Checklist

When reviewing or writing code, ensure:
- [ ] TypeScript types are properly defined
- [ ] Request/response adapters are used
- [ ] ESLint rules are followed
- [ ] Tailwind classes are properly ordered
- [ ] i18n keys are used for user-facing text (and added to locale files)
- [ ] Components use `<script setup lang="ts">`
- [ ] Props and emits are typed with TypeScript generics
- [ ] Composables follow the established pattern
- [ ] Stores use the `api` utility with adapters
- [ ] Form validation uses Yup schemas
- [ ] Error handling is implemented
- [ ] Code is organized in appropriate directories
- [ ] Route meta includes permission and module for protected routes
- [ ] Popups use `defineModel` for v-model binding
- [ ] List pages use `usePagination` and `useLoading` composables

